---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { linkTree, tileLinkTree } from "../../../demos/link";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));


const tileLinkVanilla = `import { createTileLink } from "@skryensya/vanilla/tile-link";

const link = createTileLink({ href: "/usage" });
link.textContent = "Usege details";`;

---

<Base title="Link" description="Link: a text link, always underlined, the only treatment that WCAG 1.4.1 allows.">
  <h1>Link</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Link preserves the native semantics of <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>: use it for navigation and provide a valid <code>href</code>. It is a <strong>text link</strong> with permanent underlining, the only treatment that WCAG 1.4.1 allows in prose. The hover uses the state layer (<code>sk-interactive</code>), not a color invented by the component.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={linkTree(t, "/en/components/link")} label="Link" />

  <h2>Surface Link: TileLink</h2>
  <p>
    When an entire surface is a single destination, use <code>TileLink</code>. It also renders a <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, but it is not <code>sk-link</code>: it preserves the Tile geometry and its state layer because the context, not a prose underline, communicates that the surface is navigable.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tileLinkTree(t, "/en/components/link")} label="TileLink" />
  <p>Authored HTML works without initialization. <code>createTileLink</code> only creates the anchor when the tree is generated from JavaScript.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={tileLinkVanilla} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>Why only one type, and not three</h2>
  <p>
    A link within a text block <strong>cannot be distinguished by color only</strong>: it is <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color.html">WCAG 1.4.1 (Use of Color)</a>, level A. There are only two ways to comply: a non-color indicator<em>at rest</em>, a permanent underline, or a contrast of ≥3:1 between the color of the link and that of the text <em>plus</em> a non-color signal on hover <em>and</em> focus.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second way is fragile: you have to re-measure that 3:1 in each brand and each mode, and whoever reads the page still, or cannot hover, does not receive any signal until touching the link. An underline that appears only on hover leaves the text at rest without a clue, and without an underline the flat criterion is failed. So <code>sk-link</code> has <strong>a</strong> form, always underlined, and not a hook to turn it off: offering <code>hover</code> or <code>none</code> would be offering a way to fail 1.4.1.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      <strong>What about the links that are not underlined? </strong> A navigation item, a breadcrumb, the prev/next footer, those are distinguished by <em>location</em>, not by color, so 1.4.1 does not ask for underlining. But they are not <code>sk-link</code>: they use the <code>nav-list</code> pattern or a <code>ghost</code> button. <code>sk-link</code> is, by definition, the <em>link within the</em> text.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The tone does not change the rule</h2>
  <p>
    By default the link takes the same color <strong>as the text</strong> and relies entirely on the underline. <code>data-tone="primary"</code> paints it the primary brand color. In both cases underlining is mandatory: without it, the primary tone would only be distinguished by color (WCAG 1.4.1 bug), and the default would not be distinguished from the prose.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Renders a native anchor: <code>href</code> defines the target.</li>
    <li><code>data-tone</code> is optional: without it, the text color; <code>primary</code> uses the primary brand color.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The underline is not configurable</strong>: it is always there, because a text link without permanent non-color signal fails WCAG 1.4.1.
    </li>
    <li>Does not set <code>target</code>, <code>rel</code>, or any behavior for external links.</li>
    <li>
      Takes <code>sk-interactive</code>: hover, press and focus come from the state layer. The <code>:: before</code> opens up a little more than the ink so that the wash does not read as a smear over the glyphs; the permanent underline remains the idle signal.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
